WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.830 --> 00:00:05.100
As more and more people around the world go
into self-isolation and quarantine due to

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:09.310
the ongoing coronavirus outbreak, there has
been an understandable surge in interest in

00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:10.310
pandemic movies.

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:14.849
There have been a few good ones over the years,
like the '90s disaster flick Outbreak and

00:00:14.849 --> 00:00:19.340
the intelligent take on the zombie genre 28
Days Later, but few seem to be capturing the

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:24.289
public's attention right now quite as much
as Steven Soderbergh's 2011 horrifying ensemble

00:00:24.289 --> 00:00:25.580
thriller Contagion.

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:27.580
"I think I'm sick."

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:28.580
"What?

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:29.580
What's going on?

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:32.290
What kind of symptoms do you have?"

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:33.870
"Can't swallow.

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:35.440
Severe headache."

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:40.650
Believe it or not, the movie is #2 on iTunes'
rental charts, as of the making of this video,

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:43.120
only bested by Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:45.950
Not bad for a movie that came out almost a
decade ago.

00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:50.930
For many, the draw to Contagion as their viral-outbreak
movie du jour likely has a lot to do with

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:53.210
how realistic the film set out to be.

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:57.420
Instead of delivering outlandish Hollywood
thrills, screenwriter Scott Z. Burns wanted

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:01.510
to create a realistic film that drew from
the experiences of actual people who dealt

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:02.510
with pandemics.

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:07.450
Burns interviewed epidemiologists, virologists,
and other experts in an attempt to create

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:10.040
a fictional plague that would feel true to
life.

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:13.120
We can safely say that he did a terrifyingly
good job.

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:17.240
Given how close to reality Contagion often
feels, there's a lot of interest in how this

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:19.790
fictional worldwide outbreak comes to an end.

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:22.610
So let's take a look at how Soderbergh's film
concludes.

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:27.780
Contagion begins on day two of a global pandemic
caused by a fictional viral infection eventually

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:28.780
dubbed MEV-1.

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:34.500
The event of the film kick off when Minneapolis-based
business executive Beth travels to Hong Kong

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:39.310
and Macau for work and becomes infected with
the novel virus before traveling back to the

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:40.310
U.S.

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:44.020
After Beth dies of the illness and her son
passes a few days later, there are already

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:47.170
dozens of other MEV-1 cases around the world.

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:51.340
By the time scientists realize what's going
on, it has already spread out of control.

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:56.060
The ensemble film primarily focuses on the
doctors and scientists working to understand

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:58.030
and contain the viral outbreak.

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:02.140
Doctors Ellis Cheever and Ally Hextall of
the Centers for Disease Control attempt to

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:07.990
reduce the spread of MEV-1 within the U.S.
and work to develop a vaccine, while Dr. Leonora

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:12.580
Orantes of the World Health Organization travels
to Hong Kong in an attempt to track down the

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:17.060
origins of the virus and learn how it was
able to spread out from southeast Asia and

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:18.500
infect the rest of the world.

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:23.530
After tracking Beth's movements during her
trip to Asia, Dr. Orantes is able to determine

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:28.560
on day 14 of the pandemic that Beth was not
only patient zero of the outbreak in the U.S.,

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:29.990
but also of the virus itself.

00:02:29.990 --> 00:02:34.810
However, at the CDC, the novel nature of the
virus has made it difficult to study.

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:39.920
By day 21, Dr. Hextall sees evidence that
the virus is mutating and becoming even more

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:40.920
deadly.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:45.220
Riots over medication, looted grocery stores,
and empty, trash-filled streets have become

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:49.819
the norm, and Dr. Hextall races against the
clock to unlock the code to a vaccine that

00:02:49.819 --> 00:02:52.900
will effectively stop the virus without first
killing the host.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:57.599
"If we even had a viable vaccine right now,
we would still have to do human trials, and

00:02:57.599 --> 00:02:58.760
that would take weeks."

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:02.420
Finally, on day 29, she cracks it and develops
a working vaccine.

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:06.520
In order to fast-track availability of her
new vaccine, Dr. Hextall conducts the first

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:10.810
human test on herself injecting it into her
own leg before going to visit her father,

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:14.739
who is infected with MEV-1, in the hospital
to test its effectiveness.

00:03:14.739 --> 00:03:19.060
With a vaccine developed, the MEV-1 outbreak
begins to wane on day 135.

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:24.110
Dr. Cheever declares Dr. Hextall a hero, but
she rejects the label and the spotlight that

00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:25.110
it would bring.

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:29.840
Instead, we see Dr. Hextall triumphantly placing
a sample of her vaccine in cold storage along

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:32.770
with the vaccines for SARS and H1N1.

00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:36.680
In the final scene of Contagion, we get a
flashback that solves the mystery of how the

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:37.800
virus originated.

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:43.440
Earlier in the film, Dr. Hextall notes that
the MEV-1 virus contains strains of both bat

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:47.690
and pig DNA, and in the flashback, we see
a colony of bats disturbed from a tree that's

00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:51.550
being bulldozed by a truck from the very company
that Beth worked for.

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:55.739
Later, one of the bats drops a piece of banana,
which came in contact with the virus, in a

00:03:55.739 --> 00:03:56.770
nearby pig farm.

00:03:56.770 --> 00:04:00.160
A pig eats the piece of banana, and is later
sold and butchered.

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:04.340
We later see the pig being prepared in the
kitchen of a fancy casino in Macau.

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:08.080
After handling the infected animal, the chef
is summoned away from his work.

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:11.960
He heads out of the kitchen without washing
up and poses for a photo with Beth.

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:14.120
This, we learn, was day one.

